//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: constant 'R' has an initialiser that needs a temporary
//
// Regression: `const R = f().field;` SIGSEGV'd the interpreter — and it did so
// at the REFERENCE, after the program had already run, so it read as a crash in
// whatever happened to be executing rather than as a bad constant (loft#744,
// reported as an abort with no source location at all).
//
// A file-scope constant is an INLINED EXPRESSION: its IR is pasted at each
// reference, into a var table that is not the one it was numbered against. The
// text case already stated the fact — "a buffer whose NUMBER is only valid where
// the constant was parsed" — and re-points it; a vector goes through
// `OpConstRef`. Those are two carriers. A call returning a STRUCT is a third:
// the initialiser holds a work-ref for the temporary, and pasting that number
// into `main` named a slot that was not there.
//
// Rather than add a third carrier-specific rebind, the whole class the walker
// cannot re-point is refused — at the DECLARATION, where the text case refuses
// too and where the constant can be named. That is also what the report asked
// for: the abort it saw had no file, no line and no mention of the constant.
// `visit_constant_vars` is exhaustive, so a new IR variant that carries a
// variable arrives there rather than silently pasting.
//
// Refused whether or not it is referenced: the declaration is what is wrong.
//
// The BOUNDARY is the point, and the cells either side of it are in
// `744-const-initialiser-slot-free.loft` — a constant that carries no slot must
// keep working, or this fix would have bought safety by removing the feature.

struct S { s_r: float }

fn items() -> vector<S> { [S { s_r: 0.3 }] }

// Returns a STRUCT, so a call to it needs a temporary in the caller's frame.
fn item_at(i: integer) -> S { items()[i] ?? S { s_r: 0.0 } }

const R = item_at(0).s_r;

fn main() {
  println("R={R}");
}
